specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:14:- An upgrade outside `{basePath}/ws` carries no id ([validated by: reads nothing from an upgrade outside the base path](../../src/transport/ws/helpers.test.ts#L30)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:15:- Reading the id never throws: a target that is not a URL, such as `//`, or an empty one carries no id ([validated by: reads nothing, without throwing, from a target that is not a URL or is empty](../../src/transport/ws/helpers.test.ts#L34)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:19:- Resume is the optional `transport.resume` config, off by default; without it, a reconnect naming an earlier id gets a new id, a `connected` frame with exactly its four original fields, and no replay ([validated by: with resume absent, answers a reconnect naming the first id with a new id and no replay](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L162)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:20:- With resume off, a socket opened at `{basePath}/ws/undefined` still completes the handshake and gets a server-minted id ([validated by: with resume off, still completes a handshake at /hal/ws/undefined and mints an id](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L393)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:24:- The owner reconnecting with the id from their first `connected` frame receives `resumed: true` and `entryCount` equal to the stored entries, followed by one `entry_upsert` per entry at its own index, equal to what is stored ([validated by: with resume on, answers the owner reconnecting with resumed true, entryCount 2 and both stored entries](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L177)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:25:- A resumed session takes the credentials of the socket that resumed it, and the socket it replaced closing afterwards leaves them alone, though that close still calls `onDisconnect`, since the hooks are per connection ([validated by: keeps the credentials of the socket that resumed a session when the socket it replaced closes](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L243)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:26:- A session open on more than one socket carries the credentials of the newest; when that socket closes, it takes those of the newest one still open, so a tab left open keeps working, and they are cleared only when the last one closes ([validated by: gives a session back the credentials of a socket still open when the socket that resumed it closes](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L262)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:27:- `onConnect` receives `{resumed}` as its second argument: `false` for the connection that started a session and `true` for each one that resumed it ([validated by: tells onConnect whether a connection started its session or resumed it](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L282)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:28:- An entry still streaming when its session is resumed, because its turn is running on the socket that started it, is replayed with `isStreaming: false` and `truncated: true`, since its deltas and commit go to that socket and a client shown it streaming would wait for a commit that never comes, while the stored entry stays as the running turn leaves it ([validated by: replays an entry whose turn is still running as finished and truncated, leaving the stored entry as it is](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L345)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:29:- A stored entry a tool suppressed is replayed as `entry_skip`, so the client sees the conversation it saw live and never the text a tool hid ([validated by: replays a suppressed entry as a skip, so the client never sees text a tool suppressed](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L309)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:30:- For that, suppression is recorded on the stored entry: an assistant entry the client was sent and then shown blank is marked `suppressed`, and keeps its text for the model's history ([validated by: marks a sent assistant entry it blanked as suppressed in the session, keeping its text](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L257)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:31:- A segment opened after suppression, which the client was only ever sent as a skip, is marked `suppressed` too ([validated by: marks a segment opened after suppression as suppressed in the session](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L266)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:32:- An answer still open when suppression begins is first committed as streamed so far, then retracted and marked like any sent answer, and the text after it opens a new entry the client is only sent as a skip, so a replay leaves the client as blank as the live stream did ([validated by: commits and retracts an answer still open when suppression begins, and skips what follows as a new entry](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L287), [validated by: replays an answer suppressed while it was still open as skips, as blank as the live client was left](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L332)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:33:- A thinking entry committed before suppression began is left unmarked, since the client kept seeing it, matching what the live stream showed ([validated by: leaves a thinking entry the client kept seeing unmarked, and marks the blanked answer](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L275)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:34:- A thought still open when suppression begins is committed as the client saw it and left unmarked, and its remaining text opens a new entry marked `suppressed` ([validated by: commits a thought still open when suppression begins as the client saw it, and skips what follows](../../src/transport/ws/messageHandler.test.ts#L308)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:38:- An id is resumed only for the stored session's own user: another user naming it receives a fresh session, `resumed: false` and `resumeFailure: 'unknown'`, with frames identical to the response for an id that was never issued, so the answer cannot be used to learn which ids exist ([validated by: answers another user naming the id exactly as it answers an id that was never issued](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L194)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:39:- An id that was never issued receives `resumed: false` with `resumeFailure: 'unknown'`, no replay and no `error` frame ([validated by: answers a never-issued id with resumed false, resumeFailure unknown, and no replay or error](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L208)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:40:- A requested id is never adopted as the key of a new session, which would be session fixation: the store then holds one session, under the id the server minted ([validated by: never adopts a requested id as a key: one session, under the id the server minted](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L221)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:44:- Whether an id is resumable is the store's `get` answer: an id it returns a session for is resumed, and one it returns nothing for starts fresh ([validated by: resumes the id its store knows and starts fresh for one it does not](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L294)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:45:- A resumed session whose socket closes before its replay is kept, never evicted: eviction erases, and it is reserved for a new session no client ever received ([validated by: keeps a resumed session whose socket closed before the replay, rather than evicting it](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L359)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:46:- A socket closing under resume still calls `onDisconnect` with its session id ([validated by: still calls onDisconnect with the session id when a socket closes under resume](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L234)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:50:- With `latest` on, a connect that names no `?sessionId=` rejoins the user's most recently active session, which the store's optional `latestFor(userId)` returns, with the same `resumed: true`, `entryCount`, replay and `onConnect` `{resumed: true}` as a named resume ([validated by: with latest on, rejoins the most recently active session of a user whose connect names none](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L410)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:51:- It starts a new session for a user who has none, and for a connect that sends `?new=1`, which is how a client starts a new conversation on purpose while a latest one exists ([validated by: with latest on, starts a new session for a user with none, and on ?new=1 even when one exists](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L426)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:52:- A connect that names an id rejoins that session, whichever is latest ([validated by: with latest on, still rejoins the session a connect names rather than the latest one](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L441)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:53:- A session `latestFor` answers for another user is never rejoined, and a `latestFor` that throws or that the store does not implement starts a new session ([validated by: starts a new session when the store's latestFor answers another user's session, throws, or is absent](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L453)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:54:- Without `latest`, a connect that names no id starts a new session, as before ([validated by: with resume on but latest off, starts a new session for a connect that names none](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L477)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:55:- `?new=1` counts only when it is exactly `1`, and only on an upgrade inside `{basePath}/ws` ([validated by: reads exactly ?new=1 inside the base path as a request for a new session, and nothing else](../../src/transport/ws/helpers.test.ts#L40)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:56:- `InMemorySessionStore.latestFor` goes by a session's newest timed entry, so the conversation the user last wrote to beats one created later, and an entry with no time, such as a thought, counts for nothing ([validated by: returns the user's session with the newest entry, not the one created last, a thought counting for nothing](../../src/infrastructure/stores/inMemoryLatestSession.test.ts#L17)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:57:- A session with no entries counts from its creation, and of two created at the same moment the later one wins ([validated by: falls back to creation time for sessions with no entries, the one created later winning a tie](../../src/infrastructure/stores/inMemoryLatestSession.test.ts#L30)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:58:- It never returns a session that has aged out, nor one belonging to another user ([validated by: returns nothing for a user whose only session aged out, nor another user's live one](../../src/infrastructure/stores/inMemoryLatestSession.test.ts#L45)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:59:- It reads a conversation of any length, folding the entry times rather than spreading them into one call ([validated by: reads a conversation too long to spread into a function call](../../src/infrastructure/stores/inMemoryLatestSession.test.ts#L57)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:60:- `MongoSessionStore.latestFor` returns the user's conversation with the newest `updatedAt`, read through `get`, so a live one is the cached object its running turn writes to ([validated by: returns the user's session saved last, as the cached object while it is live](../../src/infrastructure/stores/mongo/mongoLatestSession.test.ts#L12)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:61:- It reads the collection when its cache is cold, so a second instance or a restarted process finds the same latest conversation ([validated by: finds the latest session in the collection when its cache is cold](../../src/infrastructure/stores/mongo/mongoLatestSession.test.ts#L32)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:65:- `SessionStore.lookup?(sessionId)` is optional: a store with only the five original members still satisfies the interface, beside the shipped one that has it ([validated by: leaves a store with only the five original members satisfying the interface beside the shipped one](../../src/infrastructure/stores/sessionStoreLookupSignature.test.ts#L39)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:66:- It is named `lookup`, not `load`, so a durable store's own `load` of another shape still compiles beside it ([validated by: compiles beside a store's own load method of a different shape](../../src/infrastructure/stores/sessionStoreLookupSignature.test.ts#L48)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:67:- `InMemorySessionStore.lookup` reports a session past `maxAgeMs` as `{status: 'expired', userId}`, with no `session` ([validated by: reports a session past maxAgeMs as expired, with its owner and nothing else](../../src/infrastructure/stores/inMemorySessionLookup.test.ts#L20)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:68:- It reports a live session as `{status: 'active', session}`, with the very object `create` returned, and an id never issued as `{status: 'missing'}` ([validated by: reports a live session as active with the object create returned, and an id never issued as missing](../../src/infrastructure/stores/inMemorySessionLookup.test.ts#L28)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:69:- An expired session is reported once and then evicted, so the next lookup reads `missing`: the store keeps no tombstone ([validated by: reports an expired session once, then as missing, keeping no tombstone](../../src/infrastructure/stores/inMemorySessionLookup.test.ts#L40)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:70:- A session whose creation time was not a finite number is never reported expired ([validated by: never reports a session whose creation time was not a finite number as expired](../../src/infrastructure/stores/inMemorySessionLookup.test.ts#L52)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:71:- An expired result carries the owner's id and no entry content ([validated by: lets no entry content reach an expired result](../../src/infrastructure/stores/inMemorySessionLookup.test.ts#L61)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:72:- A connect naming its own session after it aged out gets `resumed: false`, `resumeFailure: 'expired'`, a new `sessionId` and no replay ([validated by: tells the owner of a session that aged out that it expired, with a new id and no replay](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L501)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:73:- Another user naming that expired id gets `resumeFailure: 'unknown'`, in a response identical to the one for an id never issued, so `expired` reveals nothing about an id that is not the caller's ([validated by: answers another user naming an expired id exactly as it answers an id never issued](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L516)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:74:- A connect that names no id gets no `resumeFailure` at all, even when the latest-session default finds nothing to rejoin ([validated by: sends no resumeFailure at all when the connect names no id, even with no latest session to rejoin](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L529)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:75:- Resume reads a named id with one call per attempt, the store's `lookup` when it has one and its `get` otherwise, and either way resumes a live session and answers a dead id `unknown` ([validated by: reads a named id with one call per attempt: lookup when the store has it, get otherwise](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L540)).
specs/hal-engine-session-resume/spec.md:79:- `ConnectedMessage` gains only the optional `resumed`, `entryCount` and `resumeFailure`, so a `connected` literal carrying neither still type-checks ([validated by: still types a connected frame literal that carries neither resume field](../../src/transport/sessionResume.test.ts#L401)).

